# #277: the summary writer's daemon-tick flush wrote the second still in
# progress and did not reset the accumulator, so the rest of that second was
# written again as a superset and the reader summed both. One logical second
# must contribute its events exactly once to a reader-side sum. Includes a
# bypass suite: an empty dir, a disabled writer, an unparseable file, a
# missing dir and a hand-built duplicate second must each be refused or go
# red, never read as agreement.
test_summary_tick_flush
# 2026-10-06, the owner's timer-sleep rule ("there is DB time when database
# doing smth - not just waiting for timer"). Six Timeout events are pure pacing
# sleeps and leave DB Time / AAS while staying VISIBLE; Timeout:PgSleep,
# SpinDelay, RegisterSyncRequest and VacuumTruncate stay in DB Time. The rule
# lives in src/idle_rule.c (one table, derived from resolved NAMES because the
# Timeout ids INVERT between PG13 and PG18 -- id 1 is CheckpointWriteDelay on 18
# and PgSleep on 13).
#   test_idle_accounting: the RAW compute path -- DB Time, the Timeout class row
#   as a strict subset, the named Idle sub-rows (so 807 s of
#   CheckpointWriteDelay is explained rather than anonymous), conservation
#   asserted by COMPONENT, and CPU measured on/adjacent to a pacing wait.
#   test_idle_summary: the SUMMARY path, which is what the demo's 900 s window
#   takes -- a predicate-only change would have left it reporting the old
#   number. Raw-vs-summary agreement under three filters, the writer-side v3
#   exclusion, and the accounting-version preflight that turns a refused v2 file
#   into a raw recompute instead of a plausible EMPTY window.
# Both carry a bypass suite: "time was excluded" is satisfied by 0 == 0, so the
# empty fixture, the absent-rather-than-wrong event, the clipped window, the
# starved summary read and an empty pacing mask each have their own case.
test_idle_accounting
test_idle_summary
# The AGGREGATE-vs-RAW differential gate, built BEFORE more views move onto the
# aggregate side. pgwt-server answers every UI question two ways -- from
# pre-aggregated per-second summary records (~50 ms at any width) or by
# materialising every raw event (3-5 s at millions) -- and should_use_summaries
# picks one, so the two never answer the same request and a disagreement is
# invisible. FID-2 shipped exactly that (docs/ROADMAP_AND_STATUS.md:579).
# For EVERY pgwt_compute_*_from_summaries() declared in src/compute.h this
# computes both paths over the same committed blocks and the same window and
# asserts BIT-EXACT agreement field by field -- no blanket epsilon, because a
# blanket epsilon is how this class of bug hides; every field that genuinely
# cannot be compared is excluded with its reason and its source line (N1..N11
# in the file header). The registry is checked against src/compute.h itself, so
# a new aggregate path with no differential turns this red, and a header it
# cannot read or parse REFUSES rather than reporting full coverage.
# Bypass suite: an empty dir, a missing dir, a window with no overlap, a
# partially-decodable file, a filter that matches nothing, and a header with no
# declarations must each be REFUSED -- "0 == 0" satisfies every assertion in
# the file, so non-vacuity is pinned before any agreement is asserted. Plus
# mutation probes proving each comparator can see one nanosecond, one count,
# one grid cell and one missing row.
test_agg_raw_crosscheck
# Paint-latency Phase 1 (docs/PAINT_LATENCY_PLAN.md): the per-committed-block
# transition aggregate, src/block_agg.c. `transitions` loads the full raw event
# window on every request (~0.68 s per million records) because a per-second
# summary record cannot express WHICH event followed which. A committed block
# is immutable, so a (old_event, new_event) -> {count, summed duration} table
# can be precomputed per block and MERGED; a request merges the blocks wholly
# inside the window and decodes raw only at the two edges.
# The oracle is the SHIPPING raw path, pgwt_compute_transitions(), not a copy
# and not this module's own accumulator — if both sides came from the same
# code the comparison could not fail. Bit-exact, no epsilon: 378 windows over
# a 5-block fixture, each compared pair by pair and node by node, plus merge
# associativity/commutativity over all 6 orderings, and the straddle rule (a
# transition whose old_event began in an EARLIER block is counted once, in the
# block holding the record, with its duration unclipped).
# §1 caught a real divergence before this shipped: pgwt_filter_matches()
# refuses a record whose duration exceeds its own end timestamp, and the
# aggregate did not.
# §9 goes through the REAL writer and reader: a multi-block trace on disk,
# driving the same pgwt_block_agg_window_from_reader() src/server.c's
# handle_transitions() calls, swept twice so the cache's contract ("a merged
# aggregate equals a fresh decode, to the nanosecond") is exercised rather
# than assumed. §3b/§3c pin the readout's TOTAL order on both sides, which is
# what makes a row-by-row comparison between the paths mean anything.
# Bypass suite: an empty block, an absent aggregate, a SAMPLES block, an
# uncommitted block, an unresolvable trace identity, a version mismatch, a
# rotation, a rewritten block (every header field perturbed in a loop), a
# double merge, injected allocation failures, every filter field, an
# empty/inverted/unparseable window, a frozen or freed aggregate, a window
# where NOTHING qualifies, a cache that can never answer, an overlapping
# SAMPLES block and an unopenable trace file — each must REFUSE, because
# "0 == 0" satisfies every assertion in the file.
# tests/results/block-agg/run_mutations.py breaks one guard at a time across
# src/block_agg.c and src/compute.c (18 mutations, all RED, control green). It
# earned its keep twice: it found the tie-order check vacuous (M13) and an
# unreachable guard that was deleted rather than left looking like protection
# (M18).
test_block_agg
# #294: cross_validate drops pgwt_is_idle_event() time from BOTH sides before
# any share is computed, so when the sampled tier reads less CPU than the exact
# tier the missing nanoseconds have no visible destination -- "it became
# ClientRead/idle" could only be INFERRED. --show-idle prints it, from a
# hand-built trace whose expected nanoseconds are literal constants (not a
# re-traversal of the tool's own loop). Bypass suite: an empty pacing mask must
# REFUSE (exit 4) rather than print zeros that read as measured; a genuine
# absence of idle events must say so in words; an empty dir and a
# sampled-only (no exact tier) capture must error; idle present on one tier
# only must still print a row; a missing/non-executable comparator and a
# generator too old to reach the refusal path must fail, never skip.
test_cross_validate_idle.sh
# Paint-latency plan Phase 3: the EXECUTIONS INDEX (src/exec_index.c). Six
# detail commands reload the full raw event window on every request at
# ~0.68 s per million records; this indexes execution BOUNDARIES once per
# committed block, with per-pid open-row state carried across block
# boundaries, so a request costs O(chunks + executions in window).
#   The whole safety argument is that one window query equals
#   pgwt_compute_executions over the array src/server.c actually builds
#   (markers-only prefix below `from` ++ every record in [from, to]) --
#   BIT-EXACTLY, on ten boundary fields, no epsilon. So this is a
#   differential against the real function over EVERY block split of 13
#   fixtures (424 cut sets, 21,625 fixture/split/window triples): a
#   one-block suite would never exercise carry-over at all, which is this
#   phase's whole point. Literal expectations pin the #222 double-EXEC_START
#   orphan closed by CMD_END, three orphans closed by one CMD_END, an
#   execution spanning seven blocks, one open at capture end, a window that
#   opens mid-execution, a zero-length execution, a plan discarded by
#   CMD_END, a plan whose query_id does not match, and a query_id that
#   arrives only on EXEC_END.
#   Bypass suite, because a boundary index's failure mode is answering
#   "no executions" when it simply cannot see: an empty index, an unsealed
#   one, an unknown version, a skipped or reordered block, an out-of-order
#   record, a record outside its block's bounds, a window not fully covered
#   and every injected allocation failure must each REFUSE with their own
#   reason and no rows -- plus a fixture with NO markers that must come back
#   as 0 rows with refused=OK, the one encoding of a genuinely empty window.
#   n_events/n_workers/matches_event_filter cannot come from a boundary
#   index, so they are POISONED to -1 rather than left at 0.
#   Companion: tests/exec_index_mutations.py injects all 16 silent-wrong
#   risks one at a time and requires this test to catch each (16/16); it is
#   not in this list because it recompiles the module 17 times (~16 s) and
#   is evidence, not a gate.
test_exec_index
